1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anthony_of_Portugal
2 CONT
2 CONT António, Prior of Crato (Portuguese pronunciation: [ɐ̃ˈtɔniu]; 1531 – August 26, 1595; sometimes, rarely, called The Determined, The Fighter or The Independentist), was a grandson of King Manuel I of Portugal, claimant of the Port
2 CONC uguese throne during the 1580 dynastic crisis, who was King of Portugal as António I of Portugal during 33 days in the continent in 1580, and, after the crowning of Philip II of Spain as King of Portugal, claimant to the throne un
2 CONC til 1583, in the Azores. He was a disciple of Bartolomeu dos Mártires.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT António was born in Lisbon, the illegitimate son of Prince Louis, Duke of Beja (1506–1555) and Violante Gomes. (Some argue that his parents were later married, perhaps at Évora.) His mother was long accused of being a Sephardic Je
2 CONC wess or a "new Christian" (a forced convert of Jewish or Muslim origin), but, in fact, she was a member of the minor Portuguese nobility, the daughter of Pedro Gomes from Évora. She died a nun at Santarém on 16 July 1568.
2 CONT
2 CONT Through his father, he was the grandson of King Manuel I of Portugal (1495–1521). Due to his illegitimate status, however, his claim to the throne was considered invalid. Nonetheless, his father had also been Prior of Crato, which
2 CONC  meant that he was able to marry without a pope's dispensation; so, the issue of young António's legitimacy is somewhat muddied. (His father may have married his mother, after all.)
2 CONT
2 CONT António was educated in Coimbra, and he was placed in the Order of St. John. He received the wealthy priory of Crato as an endowment. In 1571, he was named governor of the Portuguese fortification at Tangier in Morocco.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nonetheless, little is known of his life until 1578. In that year, he accompanied King Sebastian of Portugal (1557–1578) in his invasion of Morocco, and he was taken prisoner by the Moors at the Battle of Ksar El Kebir, the same b
2 CONC attle where the young king was slain. António is said to have secured his release on easy terms by concocting a fiction. He was asked the meaning of the cross of St. John that he wore on his doublet, and he replied that it was the
2 CONC  sign of a small benefice which he held from the Pope, something he would lose if he were not back in Portugal by 1 January 1579. His captor, believing him to be a poor man, allowed his release upon payment of a small ransom.
2 CONT
2 CONT Claimant to the throne of Portugal
2 CONT
2 CONT On his return to Portugal, António laid claim to the throne. But his pretension was opposed, and António’s uncle Henry, the cardinal archbishop of Évora and only surviving brother of King John III of Portugal (1521–1557), became t
2 CONC he new monarch. The cardinal was old and the last legitimate male representative of the royal line. In January 1580, when the Cortes were assembled in Almeirim (where the rightful heir of the Portuguese throne was decided), old Ca
2 CONC rdinal-King Henry died without having designated a successor. The regency of the kingdom was assumed by a governing junta composed of five members. By this time, the Portuguese throne was contested by several claimants. Among thes
2 CONC e were Catherine, Duchess of Braganza; her eleven-year-old nephew, Ranuccio I Farnese, Duke of Parma; King Philip II of Spain; and António, the Prior of Crato. The Duchess was named the legitimate heir later, after her descendants
2 CONC  obtained the throne in 1640 (through King John IV of Portugal); in 1580, though, she was but one of several possible heirs. According to feudal custom, her late older sister's Italian son Ranuccio was the closest heir, followed b
2 CONC y the Duchess herself and, only after both, King Philip, for he descended from Manuel I through a female line. As for António, although King Manuel I's grandson in direct male line, he was illegitimate.
2 CONT
2 CONT António, relying upon popular hostility to a Spanish ruler (even if Philip's mother was Portuguese), presented himself as an alternative candidate to King Philip II. He endeavoured to prove that his father and mother were married 
2 CONC after his birth, but no evidence of the marriage could be found (and whether such a marriage ever took place is still debated). António's claim, inferior to Philip II’s and the Duchess of Braganza’s, was not supported by the noble
2 CONC s or gentry; his partisans were drawn instead from the inferior clergy, the peasantry, and artisans. Philip ensured the success of his claim to the Portuguese crown by using gold from the Americas to bribe the upper classes of Por
2 CONC tugal; these aristocrats and rich believed that a personal union of the Spanish and Portuguese crowns would be highly profitable for Portugal (whose economy was then failing), which would maintain formal independence as well as au
2 CONC tonomous administration in both Europe and its empire).
2 CONT
2 CONT António tried to win the common people to his cause, cashing in the diffuse anti-Spanish Portuguese identity and comparing the current situation to the one of the 1383-1385 Crisis. Then, just as in 1580, the king of Castile invoke
2 CONC d arguments of blood nature to inherit the Portuguese throne; and like in 1580, the Master of Aviz (John), illegitimate son of King Peter I of Portugal, claimed his rights to the throne that ended in victory in the Battle of Aljub
2 CONC arrota and in the Cortes of Coimbra in 1385.
2 CONT
2 CONT Proclaims himself king
2 CONT
2 CONT In July 24, 1580, António proclaimed himself King of Portugal in Santarém which was followed by popular acclamation in several locations of the country. However, he governed in Continental Portugal for only 20 days, culminating in
2 CONC  his defeat in the Battle of Alcântara by the Spanish Habsburg armies led by Fernando Álvarez de Toledo, Duke of Alba on August 25.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the above event, he attempted to rule Portugal from Terceira Island, in the Azores, where he established an opposition government that lasted until 1583, and where he even minted coin — a typical act of sovereignty and royal
2 CONC ty. Because of that, many authors[clarification needed] consider him the last monarch of the House of Aviz (instead of Cardinal-King Henry) and the 18th King of Portugal.
2 CONT
2 CONT His government on Terceira Island was only recognized in the Azores. On the continent and in the Madeira Islands, power was exercised by Philip II, who was recognized as official king the following year by the Portuguese Cortes of
2 CONC  Tomar.
2 CONT
2 CONT Exile
2 CONT
2 CONT In early 1581, he fled to France carrying with him the crown jewels, including many valuable diamonds. He was well received by Catherine de' Medici, who had a claim of her own to the Crown of Portugal. She looked upon him as a con
2 CONC venient instrument to be used against Philip II. By promising to cede the Portuguese colony of Brazil to her and the sale of some of his jewels, António secured support to fit out a fleet manned by Portuguese exiles and French and
2 CONC  English adventurers.
2 CONT
2 CONT As the Habsburgs had not yet occupied the Azores, he sailed for them with a number of French adventurers under Philip Strozzi, a Florentine exile in the service of France, but was utterly defeated at sea by the Álvaro de Bazán, 1s
2 CONC t Marquis of Santa Cruz at the Battle of Ponta Delgada off São Miguel Island on July 25 and 26, 1582. He then returned to France and lived for a time in Ruel near Paris. Fear of assassins, employed by Philip II to remove him, drov
2 CONC e António from one refuge to another until he finally went to England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Queen Elizabeth I of England favoured him for much the same reasons as Catherine de' Medici did. In 1589, the year after the Spanish Armada, he accompanied an English expedition, under the command of Francis Drake and John Norreys
2 CONC , to the coast of Spain and Portugal. The force consisted partly of the queen's ships, and in part by privateers who joined in search of booty. António, with all the credulity of an exile, believed that his presence would provoke 
2 CONC a general rising against Philip II. However, none took place and the expedition was a costly failure.
2 CONT
2 CONT Latter days and death
2 CONT
2 CONT António soon fell into poverty. His remaining diamonds were disposed of by degrees. The last and finest was acquired by Nicholas Harlai, Seigneur de Sancy, from whom it was purchased by Maximilien de Béthune, duc de Sully. It was 
2 CONC later included in the jewels of the crown. During his last days, he lived as a private gentleman on a small pension given by King Henry IV of France. He died in Paris on August 26, 1595. He left six illegitimate sons by two differ
2 CONC ent women.[1] In addition to papers which he published to defend his claims, António was the author of the Panegyrus Alphonsi Lusitanorum Regis (Coimbra 1550), and of a cento of the Psalms, Psalmi Confessionales (Paris 1592), whic
2 CONC h was translated into English under the title of The Royal Penitent by Francis Chamberleyn (London 1659), and into German as Heilige Betrachtungen (Marburg, 1677).
2 CONT
2 CONT António continued to fight for the restoration of an independent Royal Dynasty of his country until the end of his life. He did not see the end of the Philippine dynasty and of the Iberian Union, in 1640, when a Portuguese — the g
2 CONC randson of his cousin, the Duchess of Braganza — was acclaimed king as John IV of Portugal, after a victorious coup in December 1, 1640.
